  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 228 AM EDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nLooking Sunday onward, models continue to shift towards less wet \nconditions Sunday morning with quickly diminishing PoPs through the \nafternoon. The cause of this is better resolution of the new surface \nhigh asserting itself across the northeast driving east surface flow \ninto the southeast CONUS. Subsequent CAD reduces moisture transport \nand keeps PWATs generally below 1.5\". The developed high will also \nsuppress much of the lifting associated with the weak subtropical \nshortwave. Our next best shot at any meaningful precipitation looks \nto come through the mid week timeframe. Timing of this secondary \nwave is uncertain as it will depend on when the western trough can \ndecouple from the west coast and begin transporting energy eastward. \nThe amplitude of the wave itself though will be enough to get the \nblocking high moving once more, which means an increase in \nconfidence when compared to this Sunday. The high amplitude nature \nof the wave also means a slightly increased thunderstorm (maybe \nsevere?) risk, however this will also depend on timing and forward \nmomentum.\n\nTemperatures through the period will be fair. Highs will start off \nin the 60s on Sunday (CAD) and increase to the 70s and near 80 \nthrough mid week. The temperature increase will be reliant on \nsoutherly flow ahead of the mid-week system.",
